diff --git a/garmin/README.md b/garmin/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..b11d9f70c4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/garmin/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# om-nav
+
+Connect IQ watch app showing Organic Maps turn-by-turn on a Garmin: next turn arrow, distance to it, the street being turned onto, route distance and arrival time, and a buzz per turn.
+
+Built separately from the phone app — Connect IQ has its own toolchain and the watch app is not part of the Gradle build.
+
+```
+NavigationService.onLocationUpdated()
+  └─ GarminNavBridge (android/libs/garmin/)
+       └─ Connect IQ Mobile SDK
+            └─ Garmin Connect app ──Bluetooth──▶ om-nav (watch)
+```
+
+Garmin Connect must be installed on the phone and paired with the watch; it is the only transport Garmin exposes. Messages travel over the local Bluetooth link rather than Garmin's servers, so a phone without Google Play services is fine.
+
+Two things must agree or messages go nowhere:
+
+**App id** — `om-nav/manifest.xml` and `GarminNavBridge.WATCH_APP_ID`
+**Turn constants** — `om-nav/source/TurnGlyphs.mc` and the `CarDirection` ordinals, pinned by `GarminNavStateTest` so a mismatch fails the Android build
+
+## Setup
+
+```sh
+brew install --cask connectiq connectiq-sdk-manager
+
+# One-off developer key, kept outside the repo so it cannot be committed
+mkdir -p ~/.garmin && cd ~/.garmin \
+  && openssl genrsa -out developer_key.pem 4096 \
+  && openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -outform DER \
+       -in developer_key.pem -out developer_key.der -nocrypt \
+  && chmod 600 developer_key.*
+```
+
+The cask ships no device definitions, so `monkeyc -d fr245m` fails with *Invalid device id* until you open **SdkManager**, sign in with a Garmin account, and install an SDK plus the Forerunner 245 / 245 Music devices.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+./garmin/build.sh              # fr245m
+./garmin/build.sh fr245        # non-music Forerunner 245
+./garmin/build.sh fr245m sim   # build and launch in the simulator
+```
+
+Output goes to `om-nav/bin/om-nav.prg`. `CIQ_DEVELOPER_KEY` overrides `~/.garmin/developer_key.der`. The script points `JAVA_HOME` at Android Studio's bundled runtime, since `monkeyc` is a JVM program.
+
+Regenerate the launcher icon with `python3 garmin/tools/make_launcher_icon.py`.
+
+## Test without the watch
+
+The simulator stands in for the watch and the phone side talks to it over ADB, so the whole loop runs on a desktop. Catches serialisation problems, turn-constant mismatches and layout issues before any hardware is involved.
+
+1. `connectiq`, then `monkeydo om-nav.prg fr245m` (or **Monkey C: Run App** in VS Code)
+2. Swap `IQConnectType.WIRELESS` for `TETHERED` in `GarminNavBridge.onNavigationStarted`
+
+## Install on the watch
+
+Copy the `.prg` into `/GARMIN/APPS/` over USB — no Connect IQ Store submission needed. Then start navigation in Organic Maps; the phone asks the watch to open the app, which it prompts you to accept the first time.
+
+The watch mounts as MTP, which Finder won't show. Use [Android File Transfer](https://www.android.com/filetransfer/) or `brew install libmtp`. If `mtp-detect` reports *No raw devices found* the connection is the problem, not the software — seat Garmin's charging clip fully and use a port on the machine itself rather than a dock or hub.
+
+Music-capable watches, including the 245 Music, move the file into private storage once they accept it, so the `.prg` disappearing from `/GARMIN/APPS/` is normal. A matching `.SET` file under `/GARMIN/APPS/SETTINGS/` confirms it took.
diff --git a/garmin/build.sh b/garmin/build.sh
new file mode 100755
index 0000000000..b373cf2718
--- /dev/null
+++ b/garmin/build.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Builds the om-nav Connect IQ watch app.
+#
+#   ./garmin/build.sh              # default device, fr245m
+#   ./garmin/build.sh fr245        # non-music Forerunner 245
+#   ./garmin/build.sh fr245m sim   # build, then launch in the simulator
+#
+# Requires the Connect IQ SDK (brew install --cask connectiq) and device definitions downloaded
+# through the SDK Manager. See README.md.
+set -euo pipefail
+
+DEVICE="${1:-fr245m}"
+RUN_SIM="${2:-}"
+
+HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
+JUNGLE="$HERE/om-nav/monkey.jungle"
+OUTPUT="$HERE/om-nav/bin/om-nav.prg"
+KEY="${CIQ_DEVELOPER_KEY:-$HOME/.garmin/developer_key.der}"
+
+# monkeyc is a bash wrapper around `java`, and this machine has no working JDK on PATH — only the
+# /usr/bin/java stub, which exists but fails when run, so test that java actually works rather than
+# that it is present. Android Studio's bundled runtime is the one the Android build uses too.
+if ! java -version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+  export JAVA_HOME="${JAVA_HOME:-/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home}"
+  export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH"
+
+  if ! java -version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+    echo "No working JDK. Set JAVA_HOME to one, e.g. Android Studio's:" >&2
+    echo "  export JAVA_HOME=\"/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home\"" >&2
+    exit 1
+  fi
+fi
+
+if ! command -v monkeyc >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+  echo "monkeyc not found. Install the SDK with: brew install --cask connectiq" >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
+
+if [ ! -f "$KEY" ]; then
+  echo "No developer key at $KEY. Generate one with:" >&2
+  echo "  mkdir -p ~/.garmin && cd ~/.garmin \\" >&2
+  echo "    && openssl genrsa -out developer_key.pem 4096 \\" >&2
+  echo "    && openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -outform DER \\" >&2
+  echo "         -in developer_key.pem -out developer_key.der -nocrypt" >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
+
+mkdir -p "$(dirname "$OUTPUT")"
+
+# -w warns on questionable constructs, -l 2 type checks informatively. Both are worth having on a
+# platform where a typo in a field name is otherwise a runtime crash on the watch.
+echo "Building for $DEVICE..."
+monkeyc -f "$JUNGLE" -o "$OUTPUT" -y "$KEY" -d "$DEVICE" -w -l 2
+
+echo "Built $OUTPUT"
+
+if [ "$RUN_SIM" = "sim" ]; then
+  echo "Starting simulator..."
+  open -a ConnectIQ
+  # The simulator needs a moment to come up before it will accept an app.
+  sleep 5
+  monkeydo "$OUTPUT" "$DEVICE"
+fi
